====================================================================
OBSERVATIONS
====================================================================

1. **Compound classes grow from 7 to 22 (3.1×). Edges grow 3.5×.**
   The 35 meta-R additions interact with the 14 data edges through
   profile shifts: every participant identifier now has one or more
   extra in-edges (from instance tokens), so its profile changes, so
   the compound classes containing its edges change too. The growth
   is therefore mostly "profile churn" and encoding artifacts, not
   new discovery.

2. **Class kinds split three ways: data-only, meta-only, mixed.**
   10 data-only, 5 meta-only, 7 mixed. Data-only classes represent
   the same original 14 edges, but partitioned by NEW fingerprints
   (because participant profiles shifted). Meta-only classes are
   registry / ownership internal to the pattern subsystem. Mixed are
   participant edges crossing the data/meta boundary.

3. **Meta-only structural repetition is real but predictable.**
   - Class `(Both-1-1, Both-3-1)` with 2 members. These are
     `R(p_0, p_0_i_0)` and `R(p_3, p_3_i_0)` — the ownership edges
     of the two single-instance patterns whose instances happen to
     have 3 participants (cycle, chain). Structurally the same edge
     kind: a pattern token pointing to its singleton instance where
     that instance has 3 participants.
   - The biggest meta-only class (6 edges, 1 subgraph) is
     `R(p_1, p_1_i_*)` — the 6-star rooted at p_1. This is p_1's
     ownership tree made visible as one compound class.
   - The class at `(LeftOnly-4-0, Both-1-1)` with 3 edges is
     `R(__pattern__, p_N)` for the three single-instance patterns —
     the top-level registry slice for "pattern with 1 instance."
     `R(__pattern__, p_1)` is alone in its own class because p_1 has
     6 instances and therefore a different profile.

4. **Mixed repetition is dominated by the "single edge" canonical
   form P2.** Of the six post-naming classes with >1 subgraph
   instance, five have canonical form `[(1, 0)]` as a dominant
   shape — i.e., "single directed edge" recurs across many contexts
   at the mixed boundary, driven by participant edges of p_1.
   This tells us that ADR 0009's P2 finding ("single edge is
   trivial but dominant") propagates into the meta-R layer too: the
   encoding itself produces lots of single-edge structural shapes.

5. **One emergent subgraph pattern is genuinely new.**
   The mixed class `(Both-3-1, Both-1-2)` contains 2 subgraphs.
   One has canonical `[(1, 0)]` (a single edge), the other has
   `[(1, 0), (1, 0), (1, 0)]` (a 3-spoke out-star). The star is the
   subgraph formed by p_0_i_0 → {k1, k2, k3} — i.e., the instance's
   participant tree for the 3-cycle pattern. This is a shape that
   *was not present in the original graph*: the 3-cycle became a
   3-spoke when re-encoded through its instance. Same cardinality
   (3 participants), different structure.
   Similarly, the class `(Both-2-1, Both-1-3)` contains a subgraph
   with canonical `[(1, 0), (1, 0)]` — a 2-spoke out-star. These
   are the instance-to-participants trees in meta-R.

====================================================================
KEY INTERPRETATION FOR γ (ADR 0012)
====================================================================

The feedback loop DOES produce novel structure — but most of the
novelty is *predictable from the encoding convention*, not emergent
insight. Specifically:

  - `R(__pattern__, p_N)` as a 4-spoke out-star rooted at the marker.
  - `R(p_N, p_N_i_M)` ownership edges as out-stars rooted at each pattern.
  - `R(p_N_i_M, participant)` edges as out-stars rooted at each instance.

These are all "trees of the pattern encoding." They recur because
the encoding convention produces them — not because the underlying
data has a new shape. Naming them as patterns would re-discover the
encoding, which is circular.

A small amount of genuinely new structure DOES appear: the
reinterpretation of data patterns (e.g., 3-cycle, 3-chain) as
instance-participant trees creates new canonical forms (stars of
various sizes). But these too are derivative of the encoding.

**Recommendation for γ (ADR 0012) iteration policy:**

- **Default: no iteration.** Re-running the pipeline against the
  enlarged RSet re-discovers encoding artifacts, not new phenomena.
- **Optional: scoped iteration** restricted to *data-only* and
  *meta-only* classes, with a rule that excludes any canonical form
  reducible to "k-spoke star" (which is the generic shape the
  encoding produces). This would let γ surface only structures that
  can't be explained by the encoding.
- **Or: iterate but with an explicit "artifact filter"** — a set of
  known canonical forms produced by the encoding. Any class whose
  canonical matches the artifact filter is skipped. The filter list
  is bounded and enumerable from the encoding convention itself:
  k-spoke stars for k ∈ {1, ..., max_instance_count}.
- **Independent of iteration, γ should still apply the relevance
  filter** it needs to suppress trivial patterns (ADR 0009's P2)
  even on the first pass. That is the primary γ job; iteration is
  optional sugar.

Also worth recording: the probe shows the *shape of the encoding*
concretely. If a future ADR considers changing the encoding (e.g.,
to something other than the three-shape convention), the tradeoff
involves these feedback-loop artifacts as well as the raw encoding
cost.

====================================================================
DECISION / NEXT
====================================================================

ADR 0011 probe complete. Findings recorded. γ (ADR 0012) can now be
scoped with concrete knowledge of what the feedback loop produces.

γ's minimum-viable scope (preliminary, to be finalized in ADR 0012):

  1. Relevance filter for pattern naming — at minimum, suppress
     canonical forms with ≤ 1 edge (the P2 "single edge" class)
     unless an explicit override is requested. Optionally a minimum
     instance-count threshold.
  2. Trigger policy — explicit manual for now; deferred automation
     until there is a use case requiring it.
  3. Iteration policy — default off. Document the artifact filter
     as a future option if iteration is ever enabled.
  4. Integrity — document but do not enforce the
     "participants-don't-gain-edges-among-themselves" invariant.
     Relax to opt-in if a use case requires it.

With these four scoped, γ closes the β layer. No β-internal
questions remain open at that point.
